SUMMARY:Twenty-third Amendment of the Constitution of Ireland
DESCRIPTION:The Twenty-third Amendment of the Constitution Act 2001 of the 
 Constitution of Ireland is an amendment that permitted the state to become
  a party to the International Criminal Court (ICC). It was approved by ref
 erendum on 7 June 2001 and signed into law on the 27 March 2002.\n\nElecti
 on: Ireland\nSource: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twenty-third_Amendment_
